. You don't want to overplay and beat your teammate unless you have a winning hand that you can clear.\n\nIf I *pass*, the Landlord will have to beat the Jacks. If they can't, great - my teammate takes control, and I've saved my cards. If the Landlord *does* beat them, I can decide what to do next with my Ace or my bomb.\n\nIf I play QQ or KK, I'm just wasting cards and disrupting my teammate's potential strategy. It's generally bad Dou Dizhu etiquette, unless the teammate is blocking for a reason I don't see.\n\nPlaying my bomb is a terrible idea. That would be just friendly fire.\n\nTherefore, the best play is to pass. This saves my cards for later and lets my teammate control the situation. Let's see how the Landlord responds. If they beat the Jacks, I'll be able to decide how to respond with an additional play.\n\n**Therefore, Pass is the best strategy here.** I should respect the teammate, be patient and avoid wasting my high cards. It is most likely best to play as a unit.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The current situation is that your teammate (Left) has played a pair of Jacks (♠J ♦J) to beat the Landlord\'s pair of 5s. This is a significant jump in rank, indicating that your teammate likely wants to take the lead or force out high cards from the Landlord. As a Peasant, your primary goal is to cooperate. Beating your teammate\'s hand (friendly fire) is generally discouraged unless you have a hand that guarantees a win or significantly disrupts the Landlord\'s flow while protecting your teammate. Your hand contains pairs (QQ, KK) that could beat the Jacks, but doing so would waste your resources on your ally and potentially block their strategy. The Landlord (Right) acts next; it is better to force the Landlord to spend high cards (like QQ, KK, AA, or 22) to beat the Jacks. You still hold strong control cards (Bomb 7777, Pair KK, Pair QQ, ♠2) to intervene if the Landlord plays a high pair.\n\nReason: You should not beat your teammate\'s pair of Jacks. Your teammate showed strength by raising the pair rank significantly. Passing allows you to see if the Landlord can beat the Jacks. If the Landlord passes, your teammate maintains control. If the Landlord plays higher cards, you can then decide to use your high pairs, 2, or bomb to defend. Playing QQ or KK now would likely help the Landlord by removing high cards from the peasants\' hands without costing the Landlord anything.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
